WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.621
Why this lesson comes first.

2
00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:06.104
Almost every big public claim about AI rests on a claim about scaling.

3
00:00:06.384 --> 00:00:16.325
When a lab leader says powerful systems may arrive within a few years, part of the argument is that capability has risen predictably with compute and will keep doing so.

4
00:00:16.605 --> 00:00:22.152
When a critic says progress is stalling, the argument is usually that one of those curves is bending.

5
00:00:22.432 --> 00:00:28.939
You cannot weigh either claim without knowing what the curves actually are, what was measured, and where the measurements stop.

6
00:00:29.219 --> 00:00:37.048
This elective is built around one habit: separate what was measured from what is extrapolated, and attach a date and a source to both.

7
00:00:37.328 --> 00:00:46.117
Scaling is the cleanest place to practice, because the measured part is unusually solid and the extrapolated part is unusually contested.

8
00:00:46.397 --> 00:00:49.896
You have a mathematics background, so here is the short version.

9
00:00:50.176 --> 00:01:03.403
The core empirical result is that pretraining loss (how badly a model predicts the next token on held-out text) falls as a power law in model size, data, and compute, over many orders of magnitude.

10
00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:06.072
On a log-log plot that is a straight line.

11
00:01:06.352 --> 00:01:20.880
Everything interesting is in the questions the straight line does not answer: how far it continues, how much it costs to stay on it, and how loss maps to things people care about, such as writing correct code or finishing a week-long project.
